we're doing a new music marathon on  April fifteenth as part of the festival  of arts science and technology fast  which is celebrating the 150th  anniversary of MIT and it's centered  around several very cutting-edge groups  and individuals all intersecting in  interesting ways so features the kronos  quartet and the bangin account All Stars  which I'm a member and then also Chinese  peapod virtuoso woman and then to MIT  groups gamelan Galactica which is a  Balinese percussion ensemble that that I  direct and the MIT chamber chorus all of  the groups intersect in very interesting  ways and one of the things that we're  trying to do with this concert is make  connections between the groups and bring  combinations together that are  interesting but I've never didn't done  before so the kronos quartet which has  been championing new music for 30 years  did a project with our gamma lon last  year a new piece by Christine southworth  who's an MIT alum and who started a  robot ensemble several years ago called  ensemble robot she came up with an idea  of an electronic gamelan which was then  developed by two other alums Alex  regalis and Laurel pardhu and we did  this project with Chronos super collider  Christine's piece at lincoln center out  of doors so we already had this  connection Chronos came here absolutely  love the whole vibe at MIT loved the  group loved the whole atmosphere and was  very interested in doing more work with  the gamelan and with MIT in general so  they're going to do that piece we also  got them together with todd mac /  composer and musical visionary and now  Todd is making a new piece for them that  features Noam Chomsky's voice and in  fact Noam Chomsky himself because no one  is going to appear on this concert so  those connections were made banga can is  here as part of a three-year ongoing  residency and that which started in the  fall with my opera house in Bali so  they're coming back doing a program of  signature works for  group and then to put it all together we  decided to do this incredibly  forward-thinking piece by Brian Eno  music for airports which bang Anakin has  been touring for some years and to put  all the groups together put bang in a  can with Chronos the two groups have  never played together before and then  add to it woman who is kind of in a way  the link between all the groups because  she's worked with everybody over the  years when she lived in Boston she did  several projects with the gamelan she's  worked with bang on a can she's worked  with Chronos so bringing her in to the  mix and then we needed a chorus so who  better to go to that our own people and  the students of the of the chamber  chorus I think it's a new experience for  them but they're pretty psyched having  worked with students in all sorts of  projects over the years here I've always  seen MIT students completely blend in  with professional musicians in these  kind of occasions and I have no doubt  that's going to be the case this time  too I've always just tried to follow the  MIT model of thinking individually and  following one's own interests and  following through on one's ideas in the  most complete and you know integrated  way one could sometimes that's involved  technology and when that's happened it's  been a great advantage to be here  because there's always lots of people  who know lots more about computers and  about equipment than I do sometimes it's  had nothing to do with technology and  sometimes it comes together as the years  go by a perfect example is what's  happened with the gamelan and these  robot instruments when I started the  balinese gamelan here in 1993 it was  very outside of what one would think and  MIT music program would have it's a  traditional music from another culture  it has nothing to do with anything that  you plug in to anything else or that you  write code to have performed and the one  thing to be said about is that my  colleagues liked the idea immediately  and supported me in doing it immediately  as the years went by the students that  would come into the gamelan would often  be students that had very interesting  musical Minds very interesting musical  imaginations and a lot of them  the same students that would be in the  gamelan would end up going to the media  lab to do course work there or even end  up being graduate students there such as  Alex regardless in tirana cozy you ended  up starting harmonics music and  inventing guitar hero and rockband such  as Laurel pardhu who built these  instruments many many other examples  over the years and at a certain point  you know students would come to me and  say hey would you mind if I tried to  build a little gamelan instrument just  as a project for my you know Becky class  or for something I'm doing at the Media  Lab and so there began to be this  intersection between this very  traditional music and more sort of  traditional MIT activities so things one  of the great things about this place is  that because it's so open and fluid and  because there's not this huge pressure  to kind of say oh well this means this  or this is for this right away I mean  there's room for the imagination to kind  of take hold for the students and four  faculty that I really interesting  fruitful projects develop over time that  you wouldn't expect musical marathons  are great ways to get to know a lot of  music without the pressure of a  90-minute concert every marathon I've  ever been at and I've been to a few you  always find something unexpected that  you really really love come for the you  know stay for women or come for Mulan  and stay for the Eno and either way  you're going to get something  interesting that you didn't you might  not have known about before I'm very  excited to bring these groups together  as part of the fast festival I think  it's the perfect place for it because to  me these groups by themselves represent  something very similar to what fast is  representing music that looks forward  that's rooted in the present but it's  looking to the future and bringing a  venue like fast allowing them to come  together and work together to both bring  what they do really well as well as  forging connections and making new art I  think it's the perfect expression of  everything that i love about MIT and  it's really thrilling for me to have MIT  be hosting this event  you
 

